---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import PassphraseGenerator from '@/components/tools/security/PassphraseGenerator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="passphrase-generator">
  <PassphraseGenerator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to generate a passphrase</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Choose how many words. Five is a good default. Four is acceptable behind a rate-limited
        login. Use six or more for a password manager, a laptop disk or a backup archive that an
        attacker could copy and attack with no time limit.
      </li>
      <li>
        Pick a separator. Hyphens keep the phrase readable and are accepted almost everywhere. Spaces
        are more natural to type, but some login forms remove them without a warning.
      </li>
      <li>
        Add a digit or symbol only if a form requires it. The tool shows how many bits each option
        adds, so you can see it is for compliance, not security.
      </li>
      <li>
        Generate again until you get a phrase you can picture. This is the only step where human
        judgement helps. Rejecting phrases you dislike does not reduce entropy much, if you do not
        filter for a pattern.
      </li>
      <li>
        Write it down while you learn it. A passphrase you forget and cannot recover is worse than a
        slightly weaker one you can. For most people, paper in a drawer is safe enough for the first
        week.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Why passphrases work</h2>
    <p>
      Human memory is good at words and bad at random characters. A passphrase gets its entropy from the
      number of words, not from complex characters. But a machine must choose the words. A phrase you
      invent (a song lyric, a saying, a sentence about your dog) has almost no entropy, because natural
      English is very predictable and attackers have built phrase dictionaries for years.
    </p>
    <p>
      This passphrase generator draws each word independently and uniformly from a list of exactly 1,024
      words, so each word adds exactly log2(1024) = 10 bits. The arithmetic is simple:
    </p>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Words</th>
          <th>Entropy</th>
          <th>Possible phrases</th>
          <th>Slow hash (20k/s)</th>
          <th>Fast hash (100 G/s)</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>3</td><td>30 bits</td><td>1.1 billion</td><td>7 hours</td><td>under a second</td></tr>
        <tr><td>4</td><td>40 bits</td><td>1.1 trillion</td><td>10 months</td><td>5 seconds</td></tr>
        <tr><td>5</td><td>50 bits</td><td>1.1 quadrillion</td><td>892 years</td><td>2 hours</td></tr>
        <tr><td>6</td><td>60 bits</td><td>1.2 quintillion</td><td>913 thousand years</td><td>2 months</td></tr>
        <tr><td>7</td><td>70 bits</td><td>1.2 sextillion</td><td>935 million years</td><td>187 years</td></tr>
        <tr><td>8</td><td>80 bits</td><td>1.2 septillion</td><td>958 billion years</td><td>192 thousand years</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>
      Note the "fast hash" column. If a site stores your password as an unsalted SHA-1, as many breached
      sites did, four words break in seconds. So use six words for anything that protects other
      secrets.
    </p>

    <h3>Diceware, and why this list has 1,024 words</h3>
    <p>
      The original Diceware list, published by Arnold Reinhold in 1995, has 7,776 entries because that
      is 6<sup>5</sup>: five throws of a physical die select a word with no arithmetic. Each word is
      worth 12.9 bits. This tool uses no dice, so the list has 2<sup>10</sup> words. Each word is worth
      exactly ten bits, and you can check the total entropy in your head: five words, fifty bits.
    </p>
    <p>
      The words are ordinary English, three to eight lowercase letters, with no apostrophes, no accents,
      no plural-and-singular pairs, and no words that are easy to mishear when read aloud. You can
      download the complete list from the tool above and count it yourself.
    </p>

    <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Choosing the words yourself. "Correct horse battery staple" is famous, so it is in all
        wordlists on the internet. So is any phrase you thought of because it felt random.
      </li>
      <li>
        Rejecting phrases until one "sounds right". If you filter for grammatical phrases, the search
        space becomes much smaller. To reject one or two because they are hard to type is fine. To
        reject fifty until you get a sentence is not.
      </li>
      <li>
        Thinking capitalisation adds strength. A capital first letter on each word is a rule, and
        attackers apply rules first. The tool reports zero extra bits for it.
      </li>
      <li>
        Using the passphrase again. A 60-bit passphrase used on ten sites is only as safe as the least
        careful of those ten.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Where a passphrase is the right choice</h2>
    <p>
      Use one wherever you have to type the secret from memory: your password manager's master
      password, a full-disk encryption key, a laptop or phone login, an SSH key passphrase, a
      recovery phrase for a backup archive. For all other logins (the hundreds you never type by hand),
      let the password manager generate long random strings. Use a memorable secret only where you
      must remember it.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
